Scattering by a repulsive interaction between two positively charged particles has
$
  V = (kappa)/(r) quad "with" kappa = (q Q)/(4 ppi epsilon_0),
$
so the results from the attractive inverse-square problem apply with $-k m -> kappa$. The orbits are
$
  r = (r_0)/(tilde(e) cos theta - 1)
$
with $r_0 = (l^2 m)/(kappa)$, and incoming and outgoing impact parameters are equal by conservation of $l$ and $E$. Matching the conserved energy $E = (1)/(2) m v^2$ with its value on the orbit gives the angle $phi$ through which the particle is scattered:
$
  phi = 2 arctan (kappa)/(m b v^2).
$
A small $b$ leads to large-angle scattering, so scattering experiments probe very small distances. Rutherford scattering (1911) showed that scattering experiments on atoms could be explained if all the positive charge in an atom was confined to a tiny nucleus.
